<BaseLayout title="Community cost voting privacy — Vetlas" description="How anonymous veterinary visit-cost votes are stored and counted."><div class="container doc page-head"><h1>Community cost voting</h1><p>Cost voting stores your selected range, optional exact total and submission time. You do not need an account, name, phone number or medical record.</p><h2>Remembering your vote</h2><p>A first-party, HTTP-only cookie identifies this browser for up to one year. We store a salted hash of that identifier for each facility, not the cookie value. One current vote per browser and facility is counted. Changing your vote replaces the earlier one; “Remove my vote” deletes it. Clearing cookies removes your ability to update that earlier vote.</p><h2>Abuse limits and retention</h2><p>A salted daily hash of the connecting network address limits repeat submissions. Raw IP addresses are not stored in the voting database. Daily limit records are cleared on the next write after that day. Votes older than 365 days are excluded and removed when the facility's results are requested. Hosting infrastructure may maintain its own operational logs.</p><h2>What is public</h2><p>Visitors see counts by cost range, an estimated average, and how many exact totals contributed. Individual vote records and identifiers are never returned to other visitors. Anonymous votes are self-reported and are not verified invoices or unique-person counts.</p><h2>Other services</h2><p>The directory serves optimized facility photos from Vetlas; opening a source link visits its credited public host. Map previews load Google Maps when opened. The optional Google Places integration remains separately configurable. The review form currently prepares an email draft; it does not use this voting database.</p><p><a href="/methodology/">Read the calculation methodology →</a></p></div></BaseLayout>
